PLACES=[
N('peterborough-lift-lock','Peterborough Lift Lock (Trent-Severn Lock 21)','Peterborough','on','canal','standing',44.30763,-78.30055,'site','public','drive','canal','1896–1904',
  "Lock 21 of the Trent-Severn Waterway is a pair of hydraulic boat lifts designed by Richard Birdsall Rogers, superintendent of the Trent Canal, who went to see lifts in France, Belgium and England in 1896. It opened on July 9, 1904. Two caissons sit on rams and balance each other: no outside power is needed, as the lift works by gravity alone on the counterweight principle, one caisson rising as the other falls. Wikipedia says the 19.8 m (65 ft) lift was for many years the highest hydraulic lift in the world, and that it was the first lock built of concrete and then the largest structure in the world of unreinforced concrete. It was designated a national historic site in 1979.",
  "The two caissons, each about 43 m long, riding up and down between massive concrete towers, still in use in the navigation season. A visitor centre beside it has exhibits on the building of the lock. Wikipedia says people skate on the canal below it in winter.",
  'A 1904 concrete elevator for boats that still runs on nothing but the weight of water.',
  "Trent-Severn Waterway National Historic Site, Parks Canada; the lock is at 353 Hunter Street East. Parks Canada's 2026 hours page says lockstations are not staffed after hours and that being on a lockstation between 10 pm and 6 am is prohibited. Visitor centre hours are kept on Peterborough Tourism's site and were not checked.",
  [W('Peterborough Lift Lock'),L('Parks Canada: Lock 21, Peterborough Lift Lock',PC+'trentsevern/visit/posteeclusage-lockstation/ecluse-lock-21-ascenseur-liftlock-peterborough'),
   L('Parks Canada: Trent-Severn 2026 hours',PC+'trentsevern/visit/heures-hours')],brick='no'),
N('fort-st-joseph-ruins','Fort St. Joseph ruins','St. Joseph Island','on','fort','ruins',46.06333,-83.94667,'site','open','walk','forts,burned','1796–1812',
  "The British began Fort St. Joseph on the southern tip of St. Joseph Island in 1796, after Jay's Treaty made them give up Michilimackinac; it was for a time their most westerly outpost in North America. Behind a palisade stood a blockhouse, powder magazine, bakery, council house and storehouse. On July 16, 1812 Captain Charles Roberts set out from here with soldiers, voyageurs and about four hundred Ottawa, Ojibwa, Menominee and Winnebago warriors and took Fort Michilimackinac the next day without a fight. Americans from Detroit later found St. Joseph empty and burned it with the North West Company's storehouses, and it was not rebuilt.",
  "Parks Canada calls the fort a historic ruin: archaeological remains to walk among, a visitor centre, and the Rains Point and Lapointe Point trails through woods and wetland to the Lake Huron shore. University of Toronto teams dug here in 1963 and 1964, and Wikipedia says digs still take place now and then.",
  'The burned outpost from which the British opened the War of 1812 in the west.',
  "Fort St. Joseph National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 185 Fort Road, Hilton Beach; St. Joseph Island is reached by the bridge from Highway 17. Parks Canada's hours page: June 1 to September 30, Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed for winter from October 1 (its main page also says closed Mondays and Tuesdays). Fees are on Parks Canada's fees page and were not checked.",
  [W('Fort St. Joseph (Ontario)'),L('Parks Canada: Fort St. Joseph',PC+'stjoseph'),L('Parks Canada: Fort St. Joseph hours',PC+'stjoseph/visit/heures-hours')],
  hours='June 1 to September 30: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed in winter',brick='?'),
N('fort-mississauga','Fort Mississauga','Niagara-on-the-Lake','on','fort','standing',43.26165,-79.07671,'site','public','walk','forts','1814–1816',
  "Built between 1814 and 1816 at the mouth of the Niagara River to replace Fort George, partly from the stone of the 1804 Mississauga Point lighthouse (which Wikipedia calls the first on the Great Lakes) and from bricks salvaged from the town of Newark, burned by retreating American forces in December 1813. Wikipedia says Captain Runchey's Company of Coloured Men, attached to the Royal Engineers, was set to building it. Parks Canada says it was garrisoned until 1826, rearmed after the 1837 rebellion and again in the 1860s, then fell into ruin until it was used to train recruits for both world wars as part of Camp Niagara.",
  "A square brick tower inside the remains of star-shaped earthworks; Parks Canada calls it the only remaining example of this type of fortification in Canada. The tower is closed all year, but paths, panels and the earthworks are open, and a plaque inside the walls marks the lighthouse site. A lakeshore trail of 2020 runs from the Fort George battlefield cairn to the fort.",
  'A brick tower built from the rubble of a burned town, standing in the middle of a golf course.',
  "Fort Mississauga National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 223 Queen Street, Niagara-on-the-Lake: free admission to the grounds, accessible year-round; the tower stays closed. Parks Canada warns that the site is on an active golf course: keep to the paths and watch the signs.",
  [W('Fort Mississauga'),L('Parks Canada: Fort Mississauga',PC+'fortgeorge/culture/fort-mississauga')],brick='?'),
N('don-valley-brick-works','Don Valley Brick Works (Evergreen Brick Works)','Toronto','on','industry','standing',43.68423,-79.36502,'site','public','walk','','1889–',
  "John Taylor and his brothers bought land in the Don Valley in the 1830s for a paper mill; William found good clay while digging fence-post holes, and the Don Valley Pressed Brick Company started in 1889, with the quarry at the north end and the plant at the south. Its bricks went into Casa Loma, Osgoode Hall, Massey Hall and the Ontario Legislature, and by 1907 two continuous down-draft kilns turned out 85,000 to 100,000 bricks a day. By the 1980s the clay and shale were mostly used up; the land was expropriated in 1989, the quarry became a park in 1997, and the charity Evergreen reopened the restored buildings in 2010.",
  "A grid of old kiln and factory sheds, some left open to their steel trusses, with the red-brick masonry kept as far as possible (Wikipedia), and the one chimney left of four. The quarry is now ponds and the Weston Family Quarry Garden. Hours of the different buildings vary.",
  "The yard that made the bricks of old Toronto, now a park in its own quarry.",
  "Toronto and Region Conservation Authority land leased to Evergreen; the quarry is a city park. Evergreen's pages: the Welcome Centre is closed December 25, 26 and January 1 and the buildings keep different hours; three paid lots run by Indigo, which fill on weekends; no parking on Bayview Avenue. A free shuttle bus runs from Broadview subway station, Monday to Saturday 6 a.m. to 1 a.m. and Sunday 8 a.m. to 6 p.m.",
  [W('Don Valley Brick Works'),L('Evergreen: plan your visit','https://www.evergreen.ca/evergreen-brick-works/visitor-info/plan-your-visit/'),
   L('Evergreen: getting here','https://www.evergreen.ca/evergreen-brick-works/getting-here/')],brick='yes'),
N('cataract-electric-ruins-forks-credit','Deagle mill and Cataract Electric powerhouse ruins','Caledon','on','industry','ruins',43.82131,-80.02238,'area','public','walk','','1820s–1947',
  "Wikipedia says there was a mill at the Forks of the Credit from 1820. In 1885 John Deagle bought the mill at the top of the falls, dammed the river to make Cataract Lake and turned the mill into a generating station, the Cataract Electric Company. Ontario Hydro later bought it and shut it in 1947; the dam was afterwards blown up and the lake drained. The Town of Caledon's heritage landscape report, quoted by Wikipedia, calls the mill ruins the most visible remnant of water-powered milling and of Deagle's early hydro-electric work on this stretch of the Credit.",
  "The ruins of the powerhouse and mill and of the dam, a piece of the old Dominion Road and Cataract Falls in the gorge, which the park's management plan sets aside as a historical zone with a lookout over the gorge near the powerhouse ruins. Exact spot of the ruins not checked on the aerial.",
  "A mill turned into one of Ontario's first hydro stations, then left to the river when the dam was dynamited.",
  "Forks of the Credit Provincial Park, Ontario Parks. Ontario Parks says gates control access, and day visitors must buy a vehicle permit, valid from 8 a.m. Keep to the trails; the management plan notes measures for the safety of visitors at the ruins. The Bruce Trail runs through the park. The pin is the falls; OpenStreetMap's Forks of the Credit Provincial Park lot is about 1.5 km east.",
  [W('Forks of the Credit Provincial Park'),L('Ontario Parks: Forks of the Credit','https://www.ontarioparks.ca/park/forksofthecredit'),
   L('Forks of the Credit Provincial Park management plan','https://www.ontario.ca/page/forks-credit-provincial-park-management-plan')],th=[43.82489,-80.00404],thn='Forks of the Credit Provincial Park parking',brick='?'),
N('decew-house-ruins-thorold','DeCew (DeCou) House ruins','Thorold','on','landmark','ruins',43.10778,-79.25028,'site','view','walk','burned','c. 1808–1950',
  "John DeCew (DeCou), a Loyalist from Vermont, built this two-storey limestone house about 1808 with walls 66 cm thick. While he was a prisoner of war it was the headquarters of Lieutenant James FitzGibbon's British outpost, and on June 24, 1813 (the HMdb marker's date) Laura Secord reached it after her walk from Queenston to warn of the American advance that ended at Beaver Dams. DeCew sold it in 1834; the Griffiths family lived in it until 1942, when Ontario Hydro took the land for the Lake Gibson reservoir. It stood empty above the water until fire destroyed it in 1950.",
  "The lower stone walls of the house, preserved with a flagstone floor and a plaque; the City of Thorold took it over from Ontario Power Generation in 2011 and finished restoring it that October. A bronze marker on the inside back wall tells the story (HMdb). The Laura Secord Legacy Trail of 2013 commemorates her walk.",
  "Where Laura Secord's walk ended: the burned shell of the house she was making for.",
  "Owned by the City of Thorold since 2011 (Wikipedia). OpenStreetMap maps a small lot on the Laura Secord Trail beside it; whether the ruin is open to walk into and any hours were not checked, so it is marked view from the public way.",
  [W('DeCew House'),L('HMdb: DeCou House Monument','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=56826')],th=[43.10762,-79.24962],thn='Lot on the Laura Secord Trail (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('morningstar-mill','Morningstar Mill (Mountain Mills) and Decew Falls','St. Catharines','on','industry','standing',43.11024,-79.26454,'site','open','walk','','1872–',
  "After the War of 1812 John DeCou built a stone grist mill with an overhead waterwheel at Decew Falls. Robert Chappel built a turbine-driven stone mill, Mountain Mills, on its foundation in 1872, and Wilson Morningstar ran it from 1883 to 1933, grinding flour and feed and running a sawmill. A fire gutted the mill: the city says in November 1892, Wikipedia says 1895. His family lived in the 1895 house until 1991; the city bought the site in 1983, and volunteers had the mill grinding again in 1992.",
  "The city says it is one of the few mills in Ontario with all its original equipment, working its millstones from its original water source: a Greey roller-mill system of the 1890s and a pair of millstones of about 1911. The Morningstar house of about 1895 has scalloped clapboard and gingerbread trim and is furnished as in 1932; there is also a sawmill, a turbine shed and the falls gorge, with the Bruce Trail behind.",
  'A working 1872 grist mill on the lip of an escarpment waterfall.',
  "Owned by the City of St. Catharines and run with the Friends of Morningstar Mill, 2714 Decew Road. The city's page, read November 2026, says the mill is open for the rest of the 2026 season Tuesday, Thursday, Saturday and Sunday, 9 a.m. to 2 p.m.; tours of the mill and house are free.",
  [W('Morningstar Mill'),L('City of St. Catharines: Morningstar Mill','https://stcatharines.ca/morningstarmill')],
  hours='2026 season: Tuesday, Thursday, Saturday and Sunday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m.',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('dundurn-castle','Dundurn Castle','Hamilton','on','landmark','standing',43.26947,-79.88419,'exact','open','drive','','1832–1835',
  "Allan Napier MacNab, later premier of the Province of Canada, built this Regency house of about forty rooms on Burlington Heights, finished in 1835 to designs by Robert Wetherell, on the foundations of Richard Beasley's brick home. It had gas lighting and running water. The pillars and portico were added in 1855 for his daughter Sophia's wedding. After MacNab died it was an institution for the deaf; Donald McInnes bought it in 1872 and sold it to the City of Hamilton in 1899.",
  "Restored in the late 1960s with the rooms set as in 1855, shown by costumed guides; Hamilton Civic Museums say entry to the house is by guided tour only, about an hour, and includes the Hamilton Military Museum on the grounds. A historic kitchen garden is open mid-May to mid-October. The front gates came from England; the stone pillars were cut on the Dundas mountain (Wikipedia).",
  "A future premier's 1830s villa, shown as it was in 1855.",
  "City of Hamilton, Dundurn National Historic Site, 610 York Boulevard. Hamilton Civic Museums' page: Tuesday to Sunday 12 to 4 p.m., closed Mondays, Good Friday, Canada Day, Christmas, Boxing Day and New Year's Day; free parking on site; Hamilton Public Library cards are taken for free general admission.",
  [W('Dundurn Castle'),L('Hamilton Civic Museums: Dundurn National Historic Site','https://hamiltoncivicmuseums.ca/dundurn-national-historic-site/')],
  hours='Tuesday to Sunday 12 to 4 p.m., guided tours only',brick='?'),
N('huron-historic-gaol','Huron Historic Gaol','Goderich','on','inst','standing',43.74955,-81.7086,'exact','open','drive','','1839–1972',
  "An octagonal county jail designed by the Toronto architect Thomas Young after Bentham's panopticon, built from 1839 to 1842 of stone from the Maitland valley and Michigan. The first Huron District Council met in a makeshift chamber on its third floor in 1842. Three men were hanged here; on December 7, 1869 Nicholas Melady was hanged on an outside wall, which Wikipedia calls the last public execution in Canada. It also served as a house of refuge for the poor and ill, closed as a jail in 1972 and opened as a museum, with the governor's house of the early 1900s, in 1974.",
  "The stone octagon and the governor's house beside it. The museum says the building is not wheelchair accessible.",
  "An eight-sided jail where Canada's last public hanging took place.",
  "County of Huron, 181 Victoria Street North, Goderich. The Huron County Museum's page: open May 15 to October 31; May to Labour Day Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 4:30; from the Tuesday after Labour Day weekdays 1 to 4 p.m., Saturday 10 to 4:30, Sunday 1 to 4:30; closed November to April. Adult admission $7.50.",
  [W('Huron Historic Gaol'),L('Huron County Museum: Huron Historic Gaol','https://www.huroncountymuseum.ca/huron-historic-gaol/')],
  hours='May 15 to Labour Day: Mon to Sat 10 to 4:30, Sun 1 to 4:30; to October 31: weekdays 1 to 4, Sat 10 to 4:30, Sun 1 to 4:30',brick='no'),
N('fort-malden','Fort Malden','Amherstburg','on','fort','standing',42.10748,-83.11329,'site','open','drive','forts','1795–',
  "Built by the British from 1795 to guard the Detroit frontier; Isaac Brock and Tecumseh met here to plan the siege of Detroit in 1812, and it was the British stronghold in the west through the war. The Americans occupied it and began rebuilding it near the old spot, and it was built up again in 1839 and 1840 under Richard Airey after the rebellions. Retired soldiers were settled round it under the Pensioner Scheme, and from 1859 it was the Malden Lunatic Asylum, an overflow for the Toronto asylum, before it was sold off. It was designated in 1921 and pieced together as it is now from 1946.",
  "Earthworks of the fort, an 1819 brick barrack restored to its 1839 look, and the Hough House; Wikipedia says several pensioners' cottages survive round the grounds and the King's Navy Yard. Interior of the buildings not checked beyond that.",
  'Where Brock and Tecumseh planned the taking of Detroit, later an asylum.',
  "Fort Malden National Historic Site, Parks Canada, Amherstburg. Parks Canada's 2026 hours: May 16 to June 30 Wednesday to Sunday 10 to 5; July 1 to September 6 daily 10 to 5; September 9 to October 11 Wednesday to Friday 1 to 5 and weekends 10 to 5; ticket sales end at 4:15. Fees not checked.",
  [W('Fort Malden'),L('Parks Canada: Fort Malden',PC+'malden'),L('Parks Canada: Fort Malden hours',PC+'malden/visit/heures-hours')],
  hours='2026: May 16 to June 30 Wed to Sun 10 to 5; July 1 to Sept 6 daily 10 to 5; to Oct 11 Wed to Fri 1 to 5, weekends 10 to 5',brick='?'),
N('josiah-henson-museum','Josiah Henson Museum of African-Canadian History (Dawn settlement)','Dresden','on','landmark','standing',42.5852,-82.19647,'site','open','drive','cemeteries','1841–',
  "Josiah Henson, who had escaped slavery, helped found the Dawn settlement here in 1841 on 200 acres bought as a refuge and workplace for former slaves, and in 1842 co-founded the British American Institute to teach them trades. The settlement had a sawmill whose lumber won Henson a medal in London, and a grist mill, brickyard and rope works. Harriet Beecher Stowe drew on his 1849 memoir for Uncle Tom's Cabin. The settlement declined after the Institute closed in 1868; Henson stayed and died in 1883. His house was opened to visitors in 1948 and the site, long called Uncle Tom's Cabin Historic Site, was renamed by the Ontario Heritage Trust.",
  "On two hectares: Henson's house (moved twice from its first position), built of local materials such as red oak; the Pioneer Church of about 1850 with the organ from the Dresden church where Henson preached; a third historic house, a sawmill restored in 2019, a smokehouse, an interpretive centre of 1994, and two cemeteries, Henson's family plot (21 headstones; Wikipedia says over 300 graves are believed to lie there) and the Institute's.",
  'The farm and grave of the man behind Uncle Tom, on the ground of a refuge he founded.',
  "Ontario Heritage Trust, 29251 Freedom Road (formerly Uncle Tom's Road), Dresden. The Trust's page, read November 2026, says the museum is closed for the season and opens again May 15, 2027, with groups of 15 or more by appointment meanwhile; self-guided tours; daily hours not given there.",
  [W('Josiah Henson Museum of African-Canadian History'),L('Ontario Heritage Trust: Josiah Henson Museum','https://www.heritagetrust.on.ca/properties/josiah-henson-museum')],
  hours='?',brick='?'),
N('sault-ste-marie-canal','Sault Ste. Marie Canal','Sault Ste. Marie','on','canal','standing',46.51278,-84.35139,'area','open','walk','canal','1895–',
  "The North West Company finished the first lock here in 1798; American forces destroyed it in 1814. In 1870 the United States refused the steamer Chicora passage through its locks. The present Canadian canal was finished in 1895; which Parks Canada calls the world's longest lock, the first operated by electricity and the last link in an all-Canadian route from the Atlantic to Lake Superior. The lock was about 274 m long. A wall collapsed in 1987 and the canal closed to traffic; a smaller lock for pleasure boats opened inside the old chamber in 1998.",
  "The 1.6 km canal, the old lock walls with the 1998 lock inside them, and the canal's historic buildings; which buildings can be entered was not checked. Pin is the Wikipedia point for the canal; see the pin note.",
  'Canada\'s answer to being shut out of the American locks: the first electric lock in the world.',
  "Sault Ste. Marie Canal National Historic Site, Parks Canada. Parks Canada's 2026 hours: the lock runs May 15 to June 18 daily 10 to 5:30, June 19 to September 7 daily 9 to 8:30, September 8 to October 15 daily 10 to 5:30; the visitor centre is open May 18 to October 9 (weekdays outside summer), 10 to 5. Closed for the season from October 16.",
  [W('Sault Ste. Marie Canal'),L('Parks Canada: Sault Ste. Marie Canal',PC+'ssmarie'),L('Parks Canada: Sault Ste. Marie Canal hours',PC+'ssmarie/visit/heures-hours')],
  hours='Lock 2026: May 15 to June 18 daily 10 to 5:30; June 19 to Sept 7 daily 9 to 8:30; to Oct 15 daily 10 to 5:30',brick='?'),
N('fort-wellington-prescott','Fort Wellington','Prescott','on','fort','standing',44.71314,-75.50853,'exact','open','drive','forts','1813–',
  "Begun in 1813 on Loyalist Edward Jessup's land to guard the head of the Galop Rapids, Fort Wellington was finished in 1814 as earthen ramparts with horizontal pickets and soon left to decay. A new fort was built on top of the old earthwork in 1838, during the rebellions, and it held militia stores and later housed soldiers bound for Europe. It was named a national historic site on January 30, 1920 and handed to the Dominion Parks Commission in 1923.",
  "The 1838 fort, restored to 1846, when a small detachment of Royal Artillery and a company of the Royal Canadian Rifle Regiment were stationed here. Guides show the storerooms and barracks in the blockhouse, which has a military museum on its third floor; a visitor centre of 2012 shows the wreck of a gunboat.",
  'An 1838 blockhouse fort at the head of the Galop Rapids.',
  "Fort Wellington National Historic Site, Parks Canada, Prescott. Parks Canada's 2026 hours: May 16 to June 30 Thursday to Monday 10 to 5; July 1 to September 7 daily 10 to 5; September 8 to October 12 Thursday to Monday 10 to 5; last admission 4 p.m.; closed from mid-October to mid-May. Fees not checked.",
  [W('Fort Wellington'),L('Parks Canada: Fort Wellington hours',PC+'wellington/visit/heures-hours')],
  hours='2026: May 16 to June 30 and Sept 8 to Oct 12 Thursday to Monday 10 to 5; July 1 to Sept 7 daily 10 to 5',brick='?'),
N('battle-of-the-windmill-prescott','Windmill Point tower (Battle of the Windmill)','Prescott','on','fort','standing',44.72109,-75.48721,'exact','public','drive','forts','1838',
  "In November 1838 Hunter Patriots, a secret American society, sailed from Sackets Harbor and landed at the hamlet of Newport on Windmill Point near Prescott, meaning to use it as a base for further attacks on Upper Canada. They held the tall stone windmill, about 60 ft high on a 30 ft bluff, whose walls stood up to small arms and field guns. British regulars, militia and the navy shelled it with heavy guns on November 16 and the invaders surrendered. The battlefield was designated a national historic site in 1920.",
  "The heavy stone windmill tower in its found form, later the Windmill Point Lighthouse (HistoricPlaces.ca), with views from its upper storeys across the river, and a surviving stone house in sight of it that HistoricPlaces.ca lists among the battle's remains. Interior not checked.",
  'A stone windmill that an invading army turned into a fortress.',
  "Battle of the Windmill National Historic Site, Parks Canada, on Windmill Road near Prescott. Parks Canada says the site is closed from September 2026 to May 2027 and reopens in June 2027. Friends of the Windmill: 613-925-1838. OpenStreetMap maps a lot on Windmill Road just north of the tower.",
  [W('Battle of the Windmill'),L('Parks Canada: Battle of the Windmill',PC+'windmill'),L('HistoricPlaces.ca: Battle of the Windmill','https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=13962')],th=[44.72179,-75.48712],thn='Windmill Road lot',brick='no'),
N('point-clark-lighthouse','Point Clark Lighthouse','Huron-Kinloss','on','light','standing',44.07282,-81.75729,'exact','open','drive','','1855–1859',
  "One of the six Imperial Towers that the contractor John Brown built for the Province of Canada on Lake Huron and Georgian Bay, all finished by 1859, to light the shoals about two miles off Point Clark. Parks Canada calls it a still-working limestone tower about 24 m (78 ft) high with a twelve-sided lantern. A restoration of more than $2.3 million began in 2011 and it reopened in June 2015.",
  "The stone tower and lantern, and the keeper's house beside it, a Township of Huron-Kinloss museum on the lives of lightkeepers. Wikipedia says the house and tower can be toured from mid-June to Labour Day for a fee.",
  'One of the stone Imperial Towers of Lake Huron, its keeper\'s house kept as a museum.',
  "Point Clark Lighthouse National Historic Site, 529 Lighthouse Road, Point Clark. Parks Canada says it is operated privately by the Township of Huron-Kinloss (519-395-3735) and was closed for the winter when read; season hours not checked on the township's own page.",
  [W('Point Clark Lighthouse'),L('Parks Canada: Point Clark Lighthouse',PC+'clark')],hours='?',brick='no'),
N('sharon-temple','Sharon Temple','Sharon','on','worship','standing',44.10127,-79.44188,'exact','open','drive','','1825–1832',
  "The Children of Peace, a sect led by the former Quaker David Willson, built the temple on his land from 1825 to 1832. Its master builder was Ebenezer Doan, a Quaker from Bucks County, Pennsylvania. Wikipedia says it was built square, unlike a Quaker meeting house, for dealing on the square with all people, with a door in the middle of each side, and that it rises more than 70 ft in three diminishing storeys for the Trinity. It was designated a national historic site in 1990.",
  "At the centre an ark holding a Bible open at the Ten Commandments, ringed by four pillars named Faith, Hope, Love and Charity. The 1.8 ha site has eight heritage buildings, among them Willson's study and the Ebenezer Doan house of 1819, moved from the Doan farm. Owned by the Sharon Temple Museum Society, now Sharon Museum and Gardens.",
  "A three-tiered wooden temple built by a breakaway Quaker sect for music and ceremony.",
  "Sharon Museum and Gardens, 18974 Leslie Street, Sharon; enter the parking lot (free) at the East Gwillimbury Civic Centre off Mount Albert Road. The museum's page, read November 2026, says it is closed for the season; the office is open Tuesday to Friday 10 to 4 for inquiries and scheduled visits. Adults $6.",
  [W('Sharon Temple'),L('Sharon Museum and Gardens: visit','https://www.sharontemple.ca/visit-the-museum')],hours='?',brick='?'),
N('bytown-museum-commissariat','Bytown Museum (the Commissariat Building) and the Ottawa Locks','Ottawa','on','canal','standing',45.42584,-75.69766,'exact','open','walk','canal','1827–',
  "The Commissariat Building beside the flight of locks where the Rideau Canal drops into the Ottawa River is, Wikipedia says, Ottawa's oldest remaining stone building. The Women's Canadian Historical Society of Ottawa founded the Bytown Museum in 1917 and moved it into the building in 1951; Parks Canada, its landlord, closed it from 1982 to 1985 to restore it. The building's date is not checked in a source opened here.",
  "The stone Commissariat at the foot of the Ottawa Locks below Parliament Hill, with the museum's exhibition on the beginnings of Bytown and Ottawa on its second and third floors. Original interior fabric not checked.",
  "Ottawa's oldest stone building, at the foot of the Rideau's last locks.",
  "Bytown Museum, 1 Canal Lane, Ottawa, in a Parks Canada building at the Rideau Canal's Ottawa Locks. The museum's own site would not open in this session: hours and fees not checked.",
  [W('Bytown Museum')],hours='?',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]
